The schema contains seven entity types: an Aircraft identified by icao24; a timestamped Observation; a timestamped Location and its AbsolutePoint geometry; a Velocity; an Orientation; and two separately identified Altitude measurements, one barometric and one geometric. All timestamped entities use both icao24 and time so observations from the same aircraft are not merged.

The Aircraft corresponds to bsm:Aircraft because icao24 and callsign are aircraft-tracking fields. Callsign maps to bsm:alternateName because that property explicitly covers callsigns and transponder-broadcast names. The icao24 value is used only as the aircraft subject identifier: it is not mapped to bsm:sourceIdentifier, whose row-identifier qualifier is incompatible with an aircraft identifier, nor is an AlternateIdentifier entity created because the schema provides no identifier-standard field.

The time-series row supports a bsm:Observation linked from the Aircraft by bsm:hasObservation, with time mapped to bsm:observationDateTime. The latitude and longitude columns support a separate bsm:Location and bsm:AbsolutePoint linked through bsm:hasGeographicLocation and bsm:hasGeometry.

Velocity maps to bsm:Velocity, with velocity mapped to bsm:speedMetersPerSecond. The heading column is mapped to bsm:headingAngle rather than bsm:absoluteBearingAngle: “heading” denotes the aircraft-axis direction, whereas the competing property is specifically qualified as course or track over ground. ADS-B conventions could make this semantically ambiguous, so the confidence is reduced. The vertrate column is a vertical rate, not the inclination angle of a velocity vector, and remains unmapped.

Barometric and geometric altitude are modeled as distinct bsm:Altitude entities because their column-name qualifiers identify different measurements. Both use the ontology’s generic bsm:altitudeMeters property, since no ontology property captures the barometric-versus-geometric datum distinction.

The onground and squawk fields have no suitable ontology properties. In particular, squawk is not promoted to bsm:AlternateIdentifier because no scheme or standard column establishes the structured scheme-plus-value pattern required by that class.

